  "src": "NAFS Grade 9 \u2022 Test 1 (Protecting the Red Sea)",
  "passage": "The Red Sea is one of Saudi Arabia's most valuable natural environments. Its warm waters contain colorful coral reefs, fish, sea turtles, and many other forms of marine life. Scientists consider some Red Sea coral especially important because it can survive unusually high water temperatures. This ability may help researchers understand how coral reefs can adapt to climate change. However, these ecosystems remain sensitive. Plastic waste, careless fishing, pollution, and damage from boats can threaten marine organisms. Protecting the sea therefore requires cooperation among scientists, government organizations, businesses, local communities, tourists, and people who live along the coast.\n\nSeveral projects in Saudi Arabia aim to protect coastal environments while allowing responsible tourism and development. Before construction begins in sensitive areas, experts may study coral reefs, animal populations, water quality, and coastal plants. Developers can then change their plans to reduce environmental harm. Some areas may be completely protected, while others may allow visitors under clear rules. Tourists can also contribute by avoiding coral, reducing plastic use, and following instructions from environmental guides. Even simple actions become significant when thousands of people practice them consistently throughout the year.\n\nProtecting the Red Sea is not only an environmental responsibility. Healthy marine ecosystems can support fishing, tourism, scientific research, recreation, and local employment. Damaged ecosystems, in contrast, may require decades to recover. Young people can participate through beach cleanups, school environmental clubs, awareness campaigns, and responsible behavior during coastal visits. They can also study marine science and develop future solutions. The long-term goal is not to prevent people from enjoying the Red Sea. Instead, it is to ensure that future generations can experience its beauty and benefit from its resources."
