  "src": "NAFS Grade 9 \u2022 Test 1 (A Young Inventor)",
  "passage": "Mariam enjoyed solving small problems around her home. One summer, she noticed that her family's outdoor plants needed frequent watering because the weather was extremely hot. Sometimes too much water flowed into the soil, while at other times the plants became dry. Mariam wondered whether a simple device could control the amount of water each plant received. Instead of immediately buying equipment, she began investigating how automatic watering systems worked. She watched educational videos, read several articles, and asked her science teacher questions about sensors, water pressure, and plant needs.\n\nUsing inexpensive materials, Mariam created a small system that measured moisture in the soil. When the soil became dry, the device released water for a short period. Her first model failed because the sensor gave inaccurate readings. The second model used too much electricity. Rather than abandoning the project, Mariam recorded each problem and modified her design. Her third model worked much better. It supplied water only when necessary and stopped automatically when the soil reached the required moisture level.\n\nMariam presented her invention at a school exhibition. Visitors were impressed not because the device was perfect but because she could clearly explain how she had improved it. Her teacher emphasized that invention usually involves repeated testing, errors, and adjustment. Mariam later began exploring whether solar power could operate the system. She learned that creativity is not simply having an interesting idea. Successful innovation also requires research, patience, careful observation, and a willingness to improve unsuccessful attempts."
