  "src": "NAFS Grade 6 \u2022 Test 1 (Falconry: A Living Heritage)",
  "passage": "Falconry is an ancient tradition with deep roots in Saudi history and Arabian culture. In the past, Bedouin hunters relied on trained falcons to catch game in the vast desert during winter months. Over time, falconry evolved from a vital survival technique into a prestigious cultural sport celebrated across the world. Falcons are admired for their incredible eyesight, lightning-fast flight speeds, and fierce loyalty to their handlers, who are called falconers.\n\nTraining a falcon requires trust, patience, and constant gentle care. A falconer spends weeks acclimating the bird to human company, often carrying it gently on a padded leather glove and rewarding it with fresh meat. Annual festivals, such as the King Abdulaziz Falconry Festival, attract thousands of participants and spectators from many countries. These events help keep historical traditions alive while teaching younger generations to respect wildlife and preserve desert biodiversity."
