  "src": "NAFS Grade 6 \u2022 Test 3 (The Science of Mangroves)",
  "passage": "Mangrove forests grow along coastal shorelines where land meets the saltwater of the sea. In Saudi Arabia, these remarkable green forests thrive along parts of the Red Sea and Arabian Gulf. Mangrove trees possess unique root systems that stick out above the muddy water, allowing the plants to breathe in oxygen even when flooded by high tides. Their roots also act as natural filters, removing excess salt from seawater so the tree can survive.\n\nMangrove ecosystems serve as critical coastal nurseries for marine wildlife. Young fish, shrimp, and crabs find shelter from ocean predators among the dense roots until they grow large enough to swim into open water. Furthermore, mangroves protect coastal towns by absorbing the impact of storm waves and preventing coastal soil erosion. Environmental programs under the Saudi Green Initiative are planting millions of mangrove seedlings to expand these vital green barriers."
