  "src": "NAFS Grade 6 \u2022 Test 5 (The Honeybee's Mathematical Hive)",
  "passage": "Honeybees are among nature's most extraordinary architects. When building their honeycombs, worker bees construct thousands of interlocking hexagonal cells out of beeswax. Scientists and mathematicians have studied bee honeycombs for centuries and discovered that the hexagon is the most mathematically efficient shape in geometry. It requires the least amount of wax to build while maximizing the volume of honey stored.\n\nEvery bee inside a colony has a specialized role that serves the collective good. Guard bees defend the hive entrance, nurse bees care for larvae, and scout bees search for flowering plants. When a scout bee finds nectar, she returns to the hive and performs a 'waggle dance' that communicates the precise direction and distance of the flowers relative to the sun. Through cooperation and communication, bees produce nutritious honey that humans have enjoyed since ancient times."
